<body>

<header>
<span class="group-badge">identity</span><span class="depth-badge">core</span>
<h1><span class="id">25</span>Speaker Embeddings</h1>
<p class="desc">
A few hundred floats that claim to be a person. The same vector conditions a multi-speaker
synthesiser, scores a cloned voice against its reference, and &mdash; because it identifies a human
being for life &mdash; sits inside your PHI boundary. Its characteristic failure is that it does not
only encode the speaker.
</p>
</header>

<main>

<section id="theory">
<h2>Theory</h2>

<h3>The vector, and the objective that shapes it</h3>
<p>
A speaker embedding is a fixed-length vector &mdash; 192 to 512 dimensions in current systems &mdash;
that lands close for two recordings of the same person, far apart for two people, and ignores what
was said. None of it is automatic; it all comes from the objective. The
<strong>d-vector</strong> (Variani et al., 2014) averaged the last hidden layer of a frame-level DNN
trained to classify the training speakers. The <strong>x-vector</strong> (Snyder et al., 2018) made
the pooling explicit: TDNN frame layers, then a statistics pooling layer concatenating the mean
<em>and</em> the standard deviation across all frames, then segment-level affine layers, with the
embedding tapped before the output softmax. That pooling discards word order &mdash; reverse the
frames and both statistics are unchanged &mdash; and returns one vector whatever the duration.
<strong>ECAPA-TDNN</strong> (2020) keeps the skeleton, adds squeeze-excitation channel attention,
multi-layer feature aggregation and <em>attentive</em> statistics pooling, and replaces cross-entropy
with additive angular margin softmax, which forces a margin between classes on the unit sphere rather
than merely making them separable.
</p>
<p>
The loss decides how you score. Margin-trained embeddings are compared by cosine after length
normalisation; plain-softmax x-vectors usually want LDA then PLDA, which models within- and
between-speaker variability separately so it can discount channel. Either way you end with one scalar
and a threshold, and the threshold <em>is</em> the system. Equal error rate &mdash; where false
accepts equal false rejects &mdash; is one point on a curve you should pick deliberately, because on a
healthcare line a wrongly admitted caller and a wrongly rejected patient are not the same event. Keep the geometry in view: on 128-dimensional unit vectors two independent random directions
have expected cosine 0 with standard deviation 1/&radic;d&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;0.088, so 0.45 is five
standard deviations from chance and can still be a different person. The baseline that matters is the
impostor distribution on <em>your</em> audio.
</p>

<h3>Conditioning a synthesiser, then reusing the vector as a judge</h3>
<p>
Multi-speaker TTS conditions on the same object. SV2TTS (Jia et al., 2018) trains a GE2E speaker
encoder on verification data, freezes it, and concatenates its 256-d output onto every encoder frame
before attention; VITS-family models inject it into the decoder, the flows and the duration
predictor. The attraction is data: the encoder learns from thousands of noisy verification speakers
who never sat in a recording booth.
</p>
<p>
The same embedding is then reused to <em>evaluate</em> cloning: cosine similarity between reference
and synthesis. When the encoder judging is the encoder that conditioned, the metric is partly
self-fulfilling &mdash; it rewards reproducing exactly the features that encoder is sensitive to.
Score with a second, independently trained encoder, and carry a similarity listening test beside it.
</p>

<h3>The failure mode: the embedding carries the room</h3>
<p>
A speaker embedding is trained to <em>discriminate</em>, not to isolate a voice, so every cue that
discriminates gets in: handset, codec, room, background noise, the line a speaker habitually calls
from. Clone from a reference recorded in a tiled bathroom and the synthesiser reproduces the reverb
along with the timbre, because the reverb is in the conditioning vector. Push it far enough and the
clusters in your projection are channels, not people. That nuisance is what LDA, PLDA and score
normalisation exist to suppress, and it is why an equal error rate measured on clean wideband data
tells you little about your fleet.
</p>
<p>
Telephony makes it concrete. An 8&nbsp;kHz leg discards everything above about 3.4&nbsp;kHz &mdash; a
band carrying a disproportionate share of the speaker cue in fricatives and the upper formants
&mdash; and G.711 mu-law quantises what is left. An encoder trained on 16&nbsp;kHz web video degrades
sharply on a PSTN leg; fine-tune at 8&nbsp;kHz rather than upsampling the call, which adds no
information. Latency is not in the extractor: it runs in single-digit milliseconds,
but you cannot embed audio you have not received. A 3-second enrolment costs 3 seconds
of wall clock, and below roughly 2 seconds of speech the within-speaker scatter grows fast enough to
swamp the between-speaker distance. Extract once per session and cache it and the cost never touches
the per-turn 800&nbsp;ms budget; recompute per utterance and you have bought variance for nothing.
</p>
<p>
Treat the array as regulated data. HIPAA's Safe Harbor list names voice prints among its 18
identifiers, so an embedding store linked to a patient is PHI, not de-identified telemetry, and
Illinois' BIPA counts a voiceprint as a biometric identifier requiring written consent. A password
can be rotated after a breach; a voice cannot.
</p>
</section>

<section id="visualization">
<h2>Visualization</h2>

<p>Everything the demo below reports comes out of these four functions:</p>
<pre><span class="cm"># x-vector pooling: T frames of D-dim activations -&gt; one 2D-dim vector</span>
def stats_pool(h):                    <span class="cm"># h: (T, D)</span>
    mu, sd = h.mean(axis=0), h.std(axis=0)
    return np.concatenate([mu, sd])   <span class="cm"># order-invariant -&gt; affine -&gt; e</span>

def length_norm(e): return e / np.linalg.norm(e)
def score(a, b):    return float(length_norm(a) @ length_norm(b))

<span class="cm"># the threshold IS the system: EER is where FAR(t) == FRR(t)</span>
def eer(tgt, imp):
    best = None
    for t in np.sort(np.concatenate([tgt, imp])):
        frr = (tgt &lt;  t).mean()       <span class="cm"># same speaker, rejected</span>
        far = (imp &gt;= t).mean()       <span class="cm"># different speaker, accepted</span>
        if best is None or abs(far - frr) &lt; best[0]:
            best = (abs(far - frr), t, 0.5*(far + frr))
    return best[1], best[2]           <span class="cm"># threshold, EER</span>

<span class="cm"># blending two enrolments is NOT a neutral midpoint. For unit mA,mB
# with c = cos(mA,mB) and w = (1-a)*mA + a*mB, at a = 0.5 BOTH |w| and
# cos(w_hat, mA) = cos(w_hat, mB) equal sqrt((1+c)/2) -&gt; 0.707 at c = 0:
# a 29% norm drop into territory no decoder was conditioned on, and a
# voice that clears an operating threshold against both endpoints.</span></pre>

<div class="viz-card">
<div class="viz-title">A speaker embedding space, scored end to end</div>
<div class="viz-desc">
Six speakers, several utterances each, in a 128-dimensional space. The geometry is real: every vector
is length-normalised, every score is a cosine over all <span id="npair">&mdash;</span> pairs, and the
EER is swept from those scores by the function above. The <em>audio</em> is not &mdash; this is a
synthetic space, so no number here measures any published model. Scatter falls as 1/&radic;T, the
idealisation you get if pooled frames were independent; real frames are correlated, so it falls more
slowly. Narrowband zeroes the 52 axes standing for the band above 3.4&nbsp;kHz.
</div>

